# DNS Fundamentals

## Overview
- DNS (Domain Name System) helps computers communicate using domain names instead of IP addresses
- Humans use domain names, but machines use IP addresses
- Example IP address: 104.26.10.229

## IPv4 Address
- Consists of 4 sets of numbers (0–255)
- Separated by dots (.)
- Example: 104.26.10.229
- Hard for humans to remember
 
## Domain Hierarchy 
- Domain names are organized in a hierarchical structure
  
### Levels
- Root Domain
- Top Level Domain (TLD)
- Second Level Domain

## Top Level Domain (TLD)
- Rightmost part of a domain name
- Example: tryhackme.com → .com is TLD

### Types of TLDs
- gTLD (Generic Top Level Domain)
  - .com → commercial
  - .org → organization
  - .edu → educational
  - .gov → government
- ccTLD (Country Code Top Level Domain)
  - .ca → Canada
  - .co.uk → United Kingdom

## Second Level Domain
- Name before the TLD
- Example: tryhackme.com → tryhackme is second level domain

### Rules
- Max 63 characters
- Can contain letters (a–z), numbers (0–9), and hyphens (-)
- Cannot start or end with a hyphen
- Cannot contain consecutive hyphens

## Subdomain
- Appears before the second level domain
- Example: admin.tryhackme.com → admin is subdomain

### Rules
- Separated using dots (.)
- Follows same rules as second level domain
- Max 63 characters
- Multiple subdomains can exist
- Example: test.admin.tryhackme.com

## Domain Length
- Total domain length must not exceed 253 characters

# DNS Record Types

## A Record
- Maps domain to IPv4 address
- Example: 104.26.10.229

## AAAA Record
- Maps domain to IPv6 address
- Example: 2606:4700:20::681a:be5

## CNAME Record
- Maps domain to another domain name
- Requires additional DNS lookup

## MX Record
- Specifies mail servers for a domain
- Includes priority value
- Used for email routing

## TXT Record
- Stores text-based data
- Used for:
  - Email authentication (SPF, DKIM)
  - Domain verification

# DNS Request Process

## Steps
1. User makes a DNS request
2. Computer checks local cache
3. If not found, request sent to recursive DNS server

## Recursive DNS Server
- Usually provided by ISP
- Has its own cache
- If found → returns result
- If not → starts lookup process

## Query Flow

### Root DNS Server
- Directs to correct TLD server

### TLD Server
- Provides information about authoritative server

### Authoritative DNS Server
- Stores actual DNS records
- Returns final IP address

# Response Path
- Response sent back to recursive DNS server
- Cached for future use
- Returned to client

## TTL (Time to Live)
- Time (in seconds) DNS response is cached
- Reduces repeated DNS requests
